## Scenario Breakdown

### Scenario 1 — Daily Fixture Fetcher
Runs on a scheduled trigger at 00:00 UTC every day. Makes an HTTP request to the Highlightly API to fetch today's World Cup fixtures, iterates through each match, and adds a row to the Match_Queue Google Sheet containing the Match ID, Home Team, Away Team, and UTC kickoff date. Duplicate prevention ensures the same match is never added twice.

### Scenario 2 — Lineup Post
Triggered by Apps Script 10 minutes before kickoff. Fetches confirmed starting lineups using the match ID, extracts formations, player names, and team logos, builds HTML player lists dynamically using Make's map and split functions, generates a punchy lineup caption via Groq, renders a branded 1080x1080 lineup graphic using htmlcsstoimage with a deep green gradient background and custom watermark, and posts it simultaneously to X via Buffer and Instagram via the Graph API.

### Scenario 4 — Post-Match Analysis
Triggered by Apps Script 2 hours after kickoff. Fetches full match statistics including final score, goal scorers, and team logos, generates a 120-word match analysis via Groq, converts the analysis into a punchy social media caption, renders a branded analysis graphic using htmlcsstoimage with the scoreline, team flags, scorer names, and watermark, posts it to X and Instagram, then generates a separate tournament progression graphic showing which stage the winning team advances to next, and posts that as a second update.

### Scenario 3 — Highlight Clip Engine
Triggered by Apps Script 9 hours after kickoff. Searches YouTube for match highlight clips, applies a relevance filter to extract only clips whose titles match specific criteria, and renders each qualifying clip as a short-form 9:16 vertical video with watermark overlay using Creatomate. Typically produces 2–3 Reels per match depending on relevance. Each clip gets an AI-generated caption via Groq and is published directly to Instagram as a Reel.

