## Logic Design

**Webhook Chain**
Apps Script acts as the conductor. It runs every 5 minutes on Google's servers, reads the Match_Queue sheet, checks each match's status and elapsed time, and fires the appropriate Make webhook. Make scenarios have no schedules of their own — they sit idle until a webhook arrives.

**Duplicate Prevention**
The status column in Google Sheets gates every trigger. Once a webhook fires and Apps Script writes a new status value, that condition can never be true again for the same match. The chain is strictly linear and irreversible.

**Dynamic Content**
No content is hardcoded. Team names, scores, formations, player lists, scorers, and stage information are all pulled from live APIs at runtime and injected into graphic templates and AI prompts dynamically.

**AI Prompting**
All Groq prompts are written with a consistent sports brand persona modelled on @433. System messages enforce tone, structure, and format. User messages inject live match data. Responses are parsed and split where multiple content types are needed in a single call.

